# rule.require.disputable-claims

> **every passage must give the reader a place to say *"no, and here is why"*. state the claim, and state what would settle it.**

the positive peer of `rule.forbid.subversive-prose` — that rule names ten shapes no observation settles and sorts each as shallow or squishy; this names the target all ten fall short of.

⇒ the ten shapes, the worked demo, and why a forbid list alone leaves an author with naught to write in place of the cut: `rule.require.disputable-claims.md` (the clamp pair is asymmetric on purpose).

## .the two moves

a claim is disputable when it carries both. either alone is half a claim:

- **assert a position** — a statement that could be false
  - it names a subject, a predicate, and a bound
  - a reader who disagrees can point at which of the three they reject
- **name the observation that would settle it** — the check, the file, the run, the measurement
  - so the disagreement has somewhere to go
  - ⇒ **this is the move authors drop**, and it is the one that makes the claim actionable

## .the test

> **what observation would settle this sentence?**

one you can name, over a stated position → a claim · a position with no observation → an opinion; name the check · an observation with no position → a procedure; state what you expect it to show · neither → cut it, or convert it.

two questions it is NOT, and each lets a defect through:

- 🟡 not *"could a reader disagree?"* — that grades taste, and the metaphor row below passes it
- not *"is it confident?"* — a stated uncertainty is a claim
  - *"70%, and one read of `getExitCodeClass.ts` settles it"* names a position, a bound, and an observation
  - *"this should probably mostly work"* names none of the three, and reads more assured

| the specimen | prose | a position? | what settles it? |
|---|---|---|---|
| the hedge | *"the review flow has some issues worth a look"* | **no** | — |
| the metaphor | *"it took the cheaper of two doors that both looked legitimate"* | yes | **none** |
| the mechanism | *"`runStoneGuardReviews` never calls `getStone…Unforgiven`, so the check runs at passage"* | yes | open the file |

⇒ the metaphor is the expensive instance — it reads as insight, and a disagreement test passes it. `forbid.subversive-prose` sorts it as squishy, repaired by the mechanism it stood in front of rather than by a cut.

## .when it fires

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you would write "it depends on context" | the strongest cue. name the axis it depends on, and which value you took |
| a sentence could take an "obviously" prefix and lose no content | no reader would contest it. cut it |
| you name a claim and not what would settle it | half a claim. the settler is the half that is absent |
| you would name a cheap settler where a stronger one exists | name the strongest — a weak settler is `forbid.obfuscation`'s shape |
| a paragraph survives a swap of its subject for any other | a truism. it discriminates no case |
| you feel a passage is unattackable | that is the alarm, never the goal. an unattackable passage said naught |

## .the caveat — a disputable claim may be WRONG, and that is the point

⇒ **an artifact cannot be corrected on a claim it never made.** a wrong disputable claim is refuted in one round; a wrong indisputable one is never refuted, and every reader inherits the error.

blocker: a passage that asserts no position a reader could reject · a position with no named observation that would settle it, where one exists · a truism that survives a swap of its subject · a hedge stack offered in place of a stated bound.
nitpick: a settler named where a stronger one was available.
false positive: a stated uncertainty with a bound and an observation · a literal the reader must type, which asserts no position · a verbatim quote · a measurement, which is its own settler.

⇒ see also: `rule.forbid.subversive-prose` (the ten shapes, and the depth) · `rule.forbid.obfuscation` · `rule.require.distillation` · `rule.require.elucidation`.
